---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import AuthorBio from '../components/AuthorBio.astro';
import HeroImage from '../components/HeroImage.astro';
import AffiliateNote from '../components/AffiliateNote.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import heroImage from '../assets/hero/urasenke-vs-omotesenke-hero.jpg';

const experiences = await getCollection('experiences');
const schoolStatedCount = experiences.filter(e => e.data.school && e.data.school !== 'none-stated').length;

const PUBLISH_DATE = '2026-08-02';
const dateFormatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' });

const TITLE = 'Urasenke vs Omotesenke: Do Tea Schools Matter for Visitors?';
const DESCRIPTION = `Urasenke and Omotesenke are two of Japan's three head tea schools, both descended from Sen no Rikyu. The differences — how the tea is whisked, how many times the bowl is turned, the pace of the ceremony — are real but subtle, and almost no Tokyo tourist venue tells you which school it follows. Of the ${experiences.length} experiences in our Price Index, only ${schoolStatedCount} names a school at all.`;

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Is Urasenke or Omotesenke better for a first-time visitor?',
    answer: "Neither — they're both legitimate expressions of the same tradition, and a tourist-facing session in either school will teach you the same core etiquette: how to receive the bowl, how to turn it, how to bow. The bigger factor for a first visit is the venue and host, not the school.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I request a specific tea school when I book a ceremony in Tokyo?',
    answer: "Rarely. Almost no booking page lets you filter by school, and most tourist-facing venues don't state one at all. If it matters to you, email the venue directly before booking and ask.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do most Tokyo tea ceremony experiences not mention a school?',
    answer: "Many tourist-facing sessions are run by trained staff or generalist cultural-experience companies rather than a lineage-holding teacher, so there's no specific school to name. That isn't necessarily a red flag — it just means the session is closer to a cultural introduction than a lesson inside a school's tradition.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the actual difference in how Urasenke and Omotesenke whisk tea?',
    answer: 'In Urasenke, the chasen moves quickly to build a fine, full foam across the whole surface. Omotesenke whisks far less, gathering foam only at the center and leaving a foam-free ring called mikazuki ("crescent moon") around it, so the raw flavor of the matcha comes through more directly.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  ogType="article"
  ogImage={heroImage}
  showCompareCta
>
  <HeroImage
    src={heroImage}
    alt="A glazed chawan with brushed markings holds freshly whisked matcha, set on a tatami mat beside a black lacquer dish"
    eyebrow="Lineage"
    title={TITLE}
  />

  <p class="text-sm text-warm-gray-mid mb-2">
    <time datetime={PUBLISH_DATE}>{dateFormatter.format(new Date(PUBLISH_DATE))}</time>
  </p>
  <AffiliateNote />

  {/* GEO answer block */}
  <div class="bg-sakura-light border border-border p-7 mb-8">
    <p class="font-label text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-sakura-deep mb-2">Quick answer</p>
    <p class="text-sumi leading-relaxed text-sm">
      Urasenke and Omotesenke are two of Japan's three head tea schools (with Mushanokōjisenke),
      both descended from Sen no Rikyu. The differences — how the tea is whisked, how many times
      the bowl is turned, the pace and formality of the ceremony — are real but subtle, and almost
      no Tokyo tourist venue discloses which school it follows. Of the {experiences.length} experiences
      in our Price Index, only {schoolStatedCount} names a school outright. In practice, whether the host is
      a genuine practitioner matters far more to your visit than which school they trained in.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose max-w-none text-sumi">
    <p>
      This question comes up a lot once people start researching beyond "book a tea ceremony in
      Tokyo" — usually after they've spotted "Urasenke" or "Omotesenke" mentioned on a venue page
      and wondered if it's a meaningful signal or just decoration. Short version: it's a real
      distinction, it's just not the one that will make or break your afternoon.
    </p>

    <h2>Three schools, one root</h2>
    <p>
      Urasenke, Omotesenke, and a third, smaller school called Mushanokōjisenke are collectively
      known as the <em>san-senke</em> — the "three Sen houses." All three trace back to Sen no
      Rikyu, the 16th-century tea master who codified much of what we now think of as tea
      ceremony, through his grandsons. The names literally mean "front house" (Omotesenke) and
      "back house" (Urasenke), reportedly describing the relative position of their tea rooms on
      the original family estate in Kyoto.
    </p>
    <p>
      Of the two, Urasenke is by far the larger presence internationally — most tea ceremony
      instruction outside Japan, including a good share of what's taught in the West, traces back
      to Urasenke. Omotesenke has historically kept a lower public profile and a reputation for a
      quieter, more austere style. Neither is "more authentic" than the other; they're sibling
      traditions that diverged over roughly 400 years, not an original-versus-imitation split.
    </p>

    <h2>What actually differs, practically speaking</h2>
    <p>
      The differences show up in technique, not philosophy. A few of the more visible ones:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the tea is whisked.</strong> In Urasenke, the chasen moves quickly to build a
      fine, full foam across the entire surface — genuinely creamy against the lips. Omotesenke
      whisks far less: foam gathers only at the center, leaving a foam-free ring around it called
      <em>mikazuki</em> ("crescent moon"), so the matcha's raw flavor comes through more directly.
      I've never trained under an Omotesenke teacher myself — tea etiquette doesn't really allow
      studying under two schools at once — so I'm passing this along as the account I've
      consistently heard from other practitioners, not something I've watched with my own eyes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the bowl is turned.</strong> Guests in both schools turn the chawan before
      drinking, as a small gesture of respect that avoids drinking from its "front" (the side
      facing outward, often the most decorated). The number of turns and the exact etiquette
      differ by school — small, but it's the kind of detail a practiced eye notices immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pace and formality.</strong> Practitioners often describe Omotesenke's temae
      (the host's ceremonial procedure) as slightly more measured and understated than Urasenke's,
      though both are, by any outside standard, slow and deliberate compared to daily life.
    </p>

    <h2>Does it matter for your Tokyo booking?</h2>
    <p>
      Honestly, less than you'd think. Of the {experiences.length} experiences tracked in our{' '}
      <a href="/tokyo-tea-ceremony-price-index">Tokyo Tea Ceremony Price Index</a>, only{' '}
      {schoolStatedCount} states a school by name. That's not because the other venues are hiding
      something — many tourist-facing sessions are run by trained staff or generalist cultural
      companies rather than a teacher inside a specific lineage, so there's genuinely no school to
      disclose.
    </p>
    <p>
      As we cover in{' '}
      <a href="/questions-before-booking-tea-ceremony-tokyo">10 questions to ask before you book</a>,
      "tea master" on a booking page is a marketing phrase, not a credential. If a host does
      practice within a named school, they'll usually say so without hesitation — a vague answer
      about "traditional Japanese style" is more informative than it sounds, just not in the way
      the venue intends.
    </p>

    <h2>How to tell which school (if any) a venue follows</h2>
    <p>
      Ask directly before booking, and pay attention to specificity. A host who trains formally
      will typically name their school, their teacher's lineage if pressed, or at minimum how long
      they've practiced. Our{' '}
      <a href="/tokyo-tea-ceremony-compared">comparison of all {experiences.length} Tokyo tea ceremony experiences</a>{' '}
      notes school affiliation where it's stated, alongside seating, price, and our own practitioner
      rating — it's one line in the listing, but it's usually the most telling one.
    </p>

    <h2>Should you choose a venue based on school?</h2>
    <p>
      Only if you already have a reason to care — you've studied a school yourself, or you want
      your Tokyo experience to connect to a specific lineage you've read about. For everyone else,
      I'd rather you choose based on what actually affects the visit: whether the host is a real
      practitioner at all, whether the room is a genuine tea room or a converted studio, and
      whether the session fits how much sitting, talking, and ceremony you actually want. School is
      a real distinction inside the tea world — it's just not the lever that determines whether
      your afternoon in Tokyo is worth the yen.
    </p>
  </div>

  <FaqBlock items={faqItems} />

  <div class="prose max-w-none text-sumi">
    <p class="text-sm text-sumi/70 border-t border-warm-gray pt-6">
      See also: <a href="/questions-before-booking-tea-ceremony-tokyo">10 questions to ask before
      you book</a>, our <a href="/tokyo-tea-ceremony-compared">comparison of all {experiences.length} Tokyo
      tea ceremony experiences</a>, and{' '}
      <a href="/about">more about Suzu's own Urasenke practice</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <EmailCapture />
  <AuthorBio />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    'headline': TITLE,
    'description': DESCRIPTION,
    'datePublished': PUBLISH_DATE,
    'author': {
      '@type': 'Person',
      'name': 'Suzu',
      'url': 'https://tokyoinseason.com/about',
    },
    'publisher': {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      'name': 'Tokyo in Season',
      'url': 'https://tokyoinseason.com',
    },
    'mainEntityOfPage': 'https://tokyoinseason.com/urasenke-vs-omotesenke-tea-ceremony-tokyo',
  })} />
</BaseLayout>
